    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d323">

                <pb facs="0523" n="433" sameAs="frus:frus1934v03/pg433"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">033.1100 Rogers, James H./33: Telegram</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Consul General at Hankow</hi> (<hi
                        rendition="#italic">Adams</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic">to the Secretary of
                        State</hi></head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Hankow</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1934-05-02T21:00:00+08:00">May 2, 1934—9 p.m.</date><lb/>[Received
                        May 2—8 p.m.]</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>Following for Morgenthau from Rogers. Chiang Kai-shek<anchor n="16"
                        xml:id="d323-f1"/> undertaking big program agricultural rehabilitation. He
                    stated to me Monday<anchor n="17" xml:id="d323-f2"/> that higher silver at this
                    time by further reducing agricultural prices would greatly retard this program.
                    The tendency of certain Government officials to favor Dies bill<anchor n="18"
                        xml:id="d323-f3"/> apparently based purely on resulting profit from further
                    use of cotton and wheat loan. Because of illness Finance Minister Kung I have
                    been unable to discuss this with him. [Rogers.]</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">Adams</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
    <standOff>
        <listAnnotation>
            <note corresp="#d323-f1" n="16" xml:id="d323-f1-content">
                <p>Chairman of Chinese Military Council and Commander in Chief of Army, Navy, and
                    Air Forces.</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d323-f2" n="17" xml:id="d323-f2-content">
                <p>April 30.</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d323-f3" n="18" xml:id="d323-f3-content">
                <p>Congressman Martin Dies, of Texas, introduced a bill (H. R. 7581) under which the
                    Treasury Department would accept silver in payment for exported farm surpluses;
                    the House of Representatives passed it March 19.</p>
            </note>
        </listAnnotation>
    </standOff>
